<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kiribati</h1><p class="st-meta">1979 CE – present · capital South Tarawa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pacific republic of the Gilbert, Phoenix and Line Islands and Banaba, independent from the United Kingdom since 1979 and reliant on fishing licences and aid.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a> colony was split in 1976 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> separated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a> gained independence as the Republic of Kiribati on 12 July 1979, with the Phoenix and Line Islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a> included. In September 1979 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> relinquished its claims to the Phoenix and Line Islands in a treaty of friendship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution of 12 July 1979 provides for a directly elected president, who heads the cabinet, and a unicameral Maneaba ni Maungatabu, which includes a nominated representative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabans">Banaban people</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabi-island">Rabi Island</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. The first elections after independence were held in 1982. Teburoro <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> was elected president in 1994, and the country joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1999. In 1995 Kiribati moved the International Date Line east to take in the Line Islands. Fisheries, copra and subsistence agriculture drive much of the economy, with fishing licence fees from foreign fleets and remittances important.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Anote Tong was president from 2003 to 2016 and urged citizens to prepare to migrate because of rising seas, buying land on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanua-levu">Vanua Levu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. Taneti Maamau took office in 2016 and won a second term in June 2020. In 2022 a constitutional crisis began with the suspension of the five major justices of the judiciary, and on 29 January 2023 Kiribati confirmed its intention to rejoin the Pacific Islands Forum.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kiribati.jpg" alt="Kiribati" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Hanzlan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kiribati.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1979 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>South Tarawa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Teburoro Tito, Anote Tong, Taneti Maamau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christian (Roman Catholic, Kiribati Uniting Church, Kiribati Protestant Church), Latter-day Saints, Baháʼí Faith</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gilbertese, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kiribati">Kiribati</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Kiribati, Gilbert Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=kiribati" aria-label="Kiribati on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9484 2128 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kiribati lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9804 2384L9806 2386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9806 2386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9804 2384h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9804 2384h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks South Tarawa. <a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=kiribati">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
